---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";

import PocketStepoverDiagram from "../../../../assets/courses/eng-design-fab/img/pocket-stepover-diagram.svg";
import StepdownDiagram from "../../../../assets/courses/eng-design-fab/img/stepdown-diagram.svg";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 8: Pockets, Step-Over &amp; Step-Down</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Unit 2 &mdash; Manufacturing Foundations, Week 3</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 8</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Pockets, Step-Over &amp; Step-Down</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Last Time</h2>
              <p>Profile cuts follow a line. A pocket clears out an entire area.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                That's a different problem &mdash; the bit can't just trace
                an outline once.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Clearing an Area Takes Many Passes</h2>
              <p>The bit sweeps back and forth, slightly overlapping each time, until the whole area is clear.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Step-Over</h2>
              <Image
                src={PocketStepoverDiagram}
                alt="Diagram comparing an even step-over, where overlapping passes clear the entire pocket area, against a step-over set too wide, which leaves ridges of uncut material between passes"
                width={720}
              />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Too Wide a Step-Over</h2>
              <p>
                Leaves thin ridges of material standing between passes
                &mdash; the pocket looks clear from above but isn't.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>A Different Problem: Depth</h2>
              <p>Cutting a thick panel to full depth in one plunge stresses the bit hard.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Step-Down</h2>
              <Image
                src={StepdownDiagram}
                alt="Cross-section diagram of material being cut in three progressively deeper passes before reaching final depth, with an arrow labeling the step-down distance between passes"
                width={720}
              />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why Shallow Passes Instead of One</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">Less heat and stress on the bit &mdash; lower breakage risk</li>
                <li class="fragment">Cleaner chip clearing at each depth</li>
                <li class="fragment">A broken bit mid-cut is worse than a slower job</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>One More Thing: Tabs</h2>
              <p>
                When a Profile cut fully separates a part from the stock,
                what's holding it in place at that final moment?
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Tabs</h2>
              <p>
                Small, deliberately uncut bridges that hold the part to the
                stock until you remove them by hand after the job finishes.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>No Tabs, No Warning</h2>
              <p>
                A fully-cut part can shift or come loose mid-job &mdash;
                right next to a spinning bit.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Step-over and step-down solve different problems.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                In Fusion you will find them under an operation's{" "}
                <b>Passes</b> tab &mdash; step-over as{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Stepover</b> (or{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Optimal Load</b> on an Adaptive
                operation), and step-down as{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Maximum Roughing Stepdown</b>, which
                only appears once you tick <b>Multiple Depths</b>.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Step-over is about area (side to side). Step-down is about
                depth (top to bottom). Don't confuse which one fixes which
                issue.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">Tabs are a safety feature as much as a practical one.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                In Fusion, tabs live on a <b>2D Contour</b> operation's{" "}
                <b>Geometry</b> tab &mdash; tick <b class="text-sn-theme">Tabs</b>,
                then set their width, height, and spacing.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A part that's still attached can't become a projectile
                mid-cut.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 9: Apply CAM to a Practice Piece</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Everything from these two sessions, programmed for real in
                OnShape.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: Diagnose a Bad Pocket</h3>
          <p>
            A pocket cut with too-wide a step-over comes out with visible
            ridges still standing. Explain, in your own words, why this
            happens even though the toolpath ran the whole area.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Step-Over or Step-Down?</h3>
          <p>For each problem below, name which one (step-over or step-down) is the actual fix:</p>
          <ol>
            <li>A pocket comes out with thin uncut ridges between passes</li>
            <li>A bit snaps partway through cutting a thick panel to full depth</li>
            <li>A pocket's bottom is rough and uneven, even though the sides look fine</li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Why Tabs?</h3>
          <p>
            Explain what would actually happen, step by step, if a Profile
            cut fully separated a part from stock with no tabs, while the
            job was still running.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, confirm you can explain, out loud, the actual difference between what step-over fixes and what step-down fixes.</p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>A pocket toolpath is really many overlapping profile-like passes. How does that change how you'd set one up, compared to Session 7's single profile cut?</li>
            <li>Session 9 has you apply all of this for real. What's the one thing from these two sessions you're least confident about actually setting correctly?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your pocket diagnosis, your step-over/step-down answers, your tabs explanation, and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
